WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.137
This is the technical deep
dive for Book 17 of the

3
00:00:04.137 --> 00:00:08.187
Federal Modernization Workbook,
AI Identity Governance.

4
00:00:08.187 --> 00:00:12.343
It's for identity engineers, security
engineers and platform owners,

5
00:00:12.343 --> 00:00:16.863
the people who issue the credentials AI
signs in with and watch what it does.

6
00:00:16.863 --> 00:00:21.030
The book's idea is simple: an AI
agent that can act is an account,

7
00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:23.613
and it needs what every account needs.

8
00:00:23.613 --> 00:00:27.200
An owner, a credential,
limits, and an end.

9
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.200
Part 1 is the identity and the inventory.

10
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.762
Part 2 is governing it and proving it.

11
00:00:32.762 --> 00:00:34.513
One note before we start:

12
00:00:34.513 --> 00:00:39.325
the product details here are dated
October 2026, and they move fast.

13
00:00:39.325 --> 00:00:41.030
Let's begin with why.

14
00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:46.167
Here's why this matters.

15
00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:50.318
A chat assistant drafts something and
a person decides what to do with it.

16
00:00:50.318 --> 00:00:51.793
An agent doesn't wait.

17
00:00:51.793 --> 00:00:55.513
It reads the mailbox, sends the
reply and closes the ticket,

18
00:00:55.513 --> 00:00:59.079
under an identity that signed
in and holds permissions.

19
00:00:59.079 --> 00:01:02.280
Only a small share of
federal AI is agentic today,

20
00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.680
but it's the share that grows fastest.

21
00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:09.118
Notice the red path: an API key
skips the identity provider,

22
00:01:09.118 --> 00:01:11.430
so no policy ever sees the call.

23
00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.568
And the risk is identity-shaped.

24
00:01:13.568 --> 00:01:16.317
A manipulated chatbot gives a bad answer.

25
00:01:16.317 --> 00:01:18.546
A manipulated agent takes an action,

26
00:01:18.546 --> 00:01:21.592
and the damage is whatever
its identity can reach.

27
00:01:21.592 --> 00:01:23.260
So, Module 1.

28
00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:28.923
Module 1 sets the ground.

29
00:01:28.923 --> 00:01:33.461
We'll look at how AI got into agencies
without the identity program noticing,

30
00:01:33.461 --> 00:01:35.983
why the controls you
already have cover it,

31
00:01:35.983 --> 00:01:40.738
the seven things every AI identity needs,
and what the federal AI rules actually

32
00:01:40.738 --> 00:01:46.080
ask of you, including the one thing people
most often get wrong about authorization.

33
00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:51.880
Most AI didn't come
through the front door.

34
00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:54.805
It came as a vendor pilot,
a key in a config file,

35
00:01:54.805 --> 00:01:58.205
or a feature switched on in
software you already had.

36
00:01:58.205 --> 00:02:03.101
So it arrived without what an employee's
account gets automatically: an owner,

37
00:02:03.101 --> 00:02:07.767
a place in the organization, a governed
credential, reviews and an end date.

38
00:02:07.767 --> 00:02:10.243
The good news is you
don't need new controls.

39
00:02:10.243 --> 00:02:13.815
The account, identifier,
authenticator and least-privilege

40
00:02:13.815 --> 00:02:17.317
controls you already meet apply
to non-person accounts too.

41
00:02:17.317 --> 00:02:19.193
The real gap is coverage.

42
00:02:19.193 --> 00:02:24.093
The AI's account just isn't in the
inventory those controls are tested against.

43
00:02:24.093 --> 00:02:26.560
Here's what closing that gap looks like.

44
00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:35.057
The program's answer is seven things
every AI identity needs: one record,

45
00:02:35.057 --> 00:02:40.045
a named human owner, a place in the
organization, credentials with no stored

46
00:02:40.045 --> 00:02:42.860
secrets, monitoring,
a lifecycle that ends,

47
00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:45.998
and evidence the authorizing
official can use.

48
00:02:45.998 --> 00:02:49.960
Each one maps to a chapter, and
to controls you already meet.

49
00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:54.035
Be clear that this is the program's
design, not a new federal rule.

50
00:02:54.035 --> 00:02:55.773
And keep the order practical.

51
00:02:55.773 --> 00:02:59.771
Start with agents, the systems that
can act, because that's where the

52
00:02:59.771 --> 00:03:03.697
damage would be, then work outward
to the systems that only answer.

53
00:03:03.697 --> 00:03:06.610
Now, what do the federal
rules actually say?

54
00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:14.879
Federal AI rules changed a lot in two
years, and one order even changed the

55
00:03:14.879 --> 00:03:19.548
vocabulary to super intelligence, though
the legal definition didn't move.

56
00:03:19.548 --> 00:03:23.336
On the left are the documents that
bind you, on the right the guidance.

57
00:03:23.336 --> 00:03:27.550
The main memo gives you a Chief
AI Officer, an annual inventory,

58
00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:31.435
compliance plans and seven
practices for high-impact AI.

59
00:03:31.435 --> 00:03:34.198
For engineers, what it
leaves out matters more.

60
00:03:34.198 --> 00:03:36.320
It creates no AI authorization,

61
00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:40.647
and it says nothing about accounts,
credentials or permissions.

62
00:03:40.647 --> 00:03:44.247
Those stay with the controls
of the system the AI runs in.

63
00:03:44.247 --> 00:03:47.485
And NIST's AI overlays
aren't even drafted yet.

64
00:03:47.485 --> 00:03:49.270
So, authorization.

65
00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:56.058
There's no AI-specific
authorization to operate.

66
00:03:56.058 --> 00:04:01.094
An AI system goes through your agency's
existing process, before it's deployed.

67
00:04:01.094 --> 00:04:04.742
What high-impact AI adds is
a second, separate signature:

68
00:04:04.742 --> 00:04:09.420
someone accepts the risk of the AI
use case after an independent review.

69
00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:12.849
That's two decisions, possibly
by two different people,

70
00:04:12.849 --> 00:04:15.058
and neither one replaces the other.

71
00:04:15.058 --> 00:04:17.859
The program ties both
to one identity record,

72
00:04:17.859 --> 00:04:22.782
so both people read the same facts about
what the system is and what it can reach.

73
00:04:22.782 --> 00:04:26.460
And if the inventory says a deployed
system has no authorization,

74
00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:28.707
that's a finding against your own policy.

75
00:04:28.707 --> 00:04:30.880
That brings us to the inventory.

76
00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:37.255
Module 2 is about knowing what you have.

77
00:04:37.255 --> 00:04:40.837
We'll read the AI use-case
inventory as identity data,

78
00:04:40.837 --> 00:04:45.503
see why a use case isn't an identity,
sort AI by whose token it presents,

79
00:04:45.503 --> 00:04:50.418
look inside Agent ID, check what each
government cloud really has today,

80
00:04:50.418 --> 00:04:53.290
and end with six kinds of AI identity.

81
00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:01.011
The inventory is the only
government-wide list of federal AI,

82
00:05:01.011 --> 00:05:04.665
but it was built to inform the
public, not to run identity.

83
00:05:04.665 --> 00:05:09.202
Read it with an identity team's eyes
and its fields sort three ways.

84
00:05:09.202 --> 00:05:11.879
A few are anchors you can key a record on,

85
00:05:11.879 --> 00:05:15.391
like a stable ID, the bureau
and the lifecycle stage.

86
00:05:15.391 --> 00:05:19.978
Some are leads you have to verify, like
the contact and the authorization answer.

87
00:05:19.978 --> 00:05:23.678
And everything that decides what an
AI can touch simply isn't there.

88
00:05:23.678 --> 00:05:27.428
Even the contact often names a
shared mailbox, not an owner.

89
00:05:27.428 --> 00:05:30.530
And the unit is wrong,
which is the next slide.

90
00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:38.005
A use case is a scenario,
and scenarios don't sign in.

91
00:05:38.005 --> 00:05:41.079
One assistant can run on
four different identities,

92
00:05:41.079 --> 00:05:45.655
and one shared platform can serve dozens
of use cases under a single identity.

93
00:05:45.655 --> 00:05:49.568
The public file doesn't even
have a stable key on every row.

94
00:05:49.568 --> 00:05:52.581
So the program puts the
join on the identity side.

95
00:05:52.581 --> 00:05:56.356
Each AI system's identity record
carries the inventory ID,

96
00:05:56.356 --> 00:06:00.367
and every directory object it
uses points back to that record.

97
00:06:00.367 --> 00:06:02.780
That same tag can later drive policy.

98
00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:06.655
Join against your agency's own
submission, not the public file.

99
00:06:06.655 --> 00:06:11.130
Next, what the inventory can say
about the high-impact practices.

100
00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:19.917
The inventory now has a field for every
one of the seven high-impact practices.

101
00:06:19.917 --> 00:06:22.668
But those fields hold answers, not proof.

102
00:06:22.668 --> 00:06:27.443
A yes for monitoring tells you the agency
believes it monitors, not what it found.

103
00:06:27.443 --> 00:06:31.604
In the published data, many high-impact
systems answered nothing, and in

104
00:06:31.604 --> 00:06:36.330
progress outnumbers yes everywhere, which
fits agencies still part way through.

105
00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:38.217
Here's the useful part for this room.

106
00:06:38.217 --> 00:06:42.340
Three practices are ones the identity
program proves as a by-product:

107
00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:46.245
monitoring from the system's own
logs, operator training checked

108
00:06:46.245 --> 00:06:49.717
against a real group, and a
fail-safe that's a tested disable.

109
00:06:49.717 --> 00:06:52.500
Now let's sort the identities themselves.

110
00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:59.859
What kind of identity an AI
has comes down to one question:

111
00:06:59.859 --> 00:07:01.625
whose token does it present?

112
00:07:01.625 --> 00:07:05.966
If it acts for a signed-in user, the
user's policies and consent apply,

113
00:07:05.966 --> 00:07:08.412
and it can do everything that user can.

114
00:07:08.412 --> 00:07:12.025
If it acts as itself, its
policy surface is thin.

115
00:07:12.025 --> 00:07:15.825
All you can do is block it, so
least privilege does the real work.

116
00:07:15.825 --> 00:07:18.445
If it has its own user
account, be careful,

117
00:07:18.445 --> 00:07:21.463
because your all-users
policies don't reach it.

118
00:07:21.463 --> 00:07:25.475
That's why Microsoft says to write
a policy for each token subject.

119
00:07:25.475 --> 00:07:30.537
And the red path again, a key outside
the identity provider, is invisible.

120
00:07:30.537 --> 00:07:32.740
Let's look inside Agent ID.

121
00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:39.190
Agent ID adds a small object model.

122
00:07:39.190 --> 00:07:42.490
The blueprint is the template,
and it holds the credential.

123
00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:44.948
The agent identities created from it hold

124
00:07:44.948 --> 00:07:47.477
permissions but no
credential of their own.

125
00:07:47.477 --> 00:07:51.339
The big consequence is that the
blueprint is a credential boundary.

126
00:07:51.339 --> 00:07:54.270
One credential serves
every agent of that kind,

127
00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:58.853
and one action on the blueprint reaches
all of them, including future ones.

128
00:07:58.853 --> 00:08:03.491
Second, what a blueprint declares
isn't granted until someone consents,

129
00:08:03.491 --> 00:08:05.928
so real access is a sum you have to read.

130
00:08:05.928 --> 00:08:09.553
Third, every agent gets a
human sponsor at creation.

131
00:08:09.553 --> 00:08:12.020
Now, which clouds actually have this?

132
00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:19.645
Your cloud decides what you can use,
and this picture changes monthly.

133
00:08:19.645 --> 00:08:21.895
The platform is generally available.

134
00:08:21.895 --> 00:08:26.566
GCC has a documented subset: you can
govern agent identity and access,

135
00:08:26.566 --> 00:08:31.483
but not yet get Microsoft's audit and
data protection for agent activity.

136
00:08:31.483 --> 00:08:35.795
GCC High and DoD have nothing
published, so treat agent identities

137
00:08:35.795 --> 00:08:39.794
as unavailable there until
Microsoft confirms it in writing.

138
00:08:39.794 --> 00:08:44.733
Licensing is its own trap, and the FedRAMP
High authorization is still pending.

139
00:08:44.733 --> 00:08:49.187
The program's rule is to build on what's
generally available in your own cloud,

140
00:08:49.187 --> 00:08:51.732
and to treat preview as
something you watch.

141
00:08:51.732 --> 00:08:54.060
So how do we classify all this?

142
00:08:57.810 --> 00:09:03.340
Putting it together, the program sorts
every AI system into six kinds of identity:

143
00:09:03.340 --> 00:09:07.633
a delegated assistant,
workload-identity AI, an agent identity,

144
00:09:07.633 --> 00:09:12.998
an agent with its own user account, a
vendor-hosted service, and an unmanaged key.

145
00:09:12.998 --> 00:09:16.763
Each kind tells you who owns
it, how it should authenticate,

146
00:09:16.763 --> 00:09:19.273
what policy can reach it, and how it ends.

147
00:09:19.273 --> 00:09:21.572
Most of the risk sits in the last two.

148
00:09:21.572 --> 00:09:25.547
A vendor's service is visible
but outside your workload policy.

149
00:09:25.547 --> 00:09:28.025
A raw key isn't in the directory at all,

150
00:09:28.025 --> 00:09:30.998
so it has no owner and no
end until you find it.

151
00:09:30.998 --> 00:09:35.210
Everything else can be governed,
and Part 2 does exactly that.

152
00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:41.373
Module 3 makes governance real.

153
00:09:41.373 --> 00:09:44.421
We'll attach every agent
to accountable people,

154
00:09:44.421 --> 00:09:47.627
run its lifecycle from the
sponsor's own HR events,

155
00:09:47.627 --> 00:09:52.240
give it credentials with no stored
secrets and only the access it needs,

156
00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:57.380
and then bound what it can call, through
policy, tool registration and data.

157
00:10:01.130 --> 00:10:06.117
An agent has no hire date, so its lifecycle
has to hang from a person who does.

158
00:10:06.117 --> 00:10:08.429
Agent ID gives you three roles.

159
00:10:08.429 --> 00:10:10.667
The owner holds the technical keys.

160
00:10:10.667 --> 00:10:13.262
The sponsor answers for
why the agent exists,

161
00:10:13.262 --> 00:10:16.768
and can switch it off but
deliberately can't switch it back on.

162
00:10:16.768 --> 00:10:20.593
The manager places an agent's
user account in the organization.

163
00:10:20.593 --> 00:10:23.032
The catch is the defaults: left alone,

164
00:10:23.032 --> 00:10:26.855
whoever built the agent ends
up sponsoring and owning it.

165
00:10:26.855 --> 00:10:29.092
So the program sets it on purpose.

166
00:10:29.092 --> 00:10:33.167
An HR-mastered federal sponsor, a
small group of stewards as backup,

167
00:10:33.167 --> 00:10:37.167
a separate technical owner, and
administrators sponsor nothing.

168
00:10:37.167 --> 00:10:39.590
Here's how that drives the lifecycle.

169
00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:45.440
The lifecycle mirrors a person's.

170
00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:49.965
Nothing goes to production without an
approved inventory entry and a handshake.

171
00:10:49.965 --> 00:10:54.790
The event that matters most is the sponsor
leaving, because the agent never does.

172
00:10:54.790 --> 00:10:59.696
Lifecycle workflows can hand sponsorship
to the manager, and the program hangs

173
00:10:59.696 --> 00:11:04.602
that off HR's own events and gives the
manager a short window to actually confirm.

174
00:11:04.602 --> 00:11:06.152
Then there's a neat inversion.

175
00:11:06.152 --> 00:11:08.849
Access comes in packages with end dates,

176
00:11:08.849 --> 00:11:13.178
so if the sponsor ignores the
renewal, the access simply expires.

177
00:11:13.178 --> 00:11:16.777
Ignoring the review removes
access instead of keeping it.

178
00:11:16.777 --> 00:11:19.090
Now, how an agent ends.

179
00:11:22.840 --> 00:11:25.037
Ending an agent takes two steps,

180
00:11:25.037 --> 00:11:29.178
and the program keeps them apart:
disable first, then delete.

181
00:11:29.178 --> 00:11:34.585
Deleting a blueprint cascades to all
its agents, slowly, and restores only

182
00:11:34.585 --> 00:11:39.903
work for thirty days, so disable, record
the decision, wait, and then delete.

183
00:11:39.903 --> 00:11:45.329
An agent is orphaned when any of its
anchors fails: no confirmed sponsor,

184
00:11:45.329 --> 00:11:50.203
no owner, no activity, no inventory
record, or a deleted blueprint.

185
00:11:50.203 --> 00:11:55.478
A person disables it, never automation,
and the office gets thirty days to decide.

186
00:11:55.478 --> 00:11:57.759
Where agent features aren't available,

187
00:11:57.759 --> 00:12:02.465
run the same lifecycle on application
objects, and say honestly that it's weaker.

188
00:12:02.465 --> 00:12:04.350
Next, credentials.

189
00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:10.851
An agent can't do
multifactor authentication,

190
00:12:10.851 --> 00:12:13.675
so its credential has to
be right from the start.

191
00:12:13.675 --> 00:12:18.087
Because the blueprint holds the
credential for every agent made from it,

192
00:12:18.087 --> 00:12:20.735
the order matters: a
managed identity first,

193
00:12:20.735 --> 00:12:24.632
federation from the agent's own
platform next, a certificate in a

194
00:12:24.632 --> 00:12:28.750
vault only if neither works, and a
client secret never in production.

195
00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:32.340
The program enforces that with
an app management policy that

196
00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:36.000
already covers blueprints, so a
production secret is a finding.

197
00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:38.925
And watch the keys an
agent uses for its tools.

198
00:12:38.925 --> 00:12:43.325
An unavoidable key needs a vault,
an owner and a rotation date.

199
00:12:43.325 --> 00:12:44.900
Now, privilege.

200
00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:52.171
Least privilege starts with
choosing how the agent acts:

201
00:12:52.171 --> 00:12:56.367
for a user when someone's present, as
itself only when there's a reason,

202
00:12:56.367 --> 00:13:00.263
and with its own user account only
when it truly needs a mailbox.

203
00:13:00.263 --> 00:13:04.728
Microsoft blocks the most dangerous
roles and permissions for agents,

204
00:13:04.728 --> 00:13:07.074
but that's a floor, not least privilege.

205
00:13:07.074 --> 00:13:10.588
Agents can still hold roles that
are privileged in your tenant,

206
00:13:10.588 --> 00:13:12.750
so apply your own risk classification.

207
00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:16.344
Keep inherited permissions
narrow, because one broad

208
00:13:16.344 --> 00:13:20.338
setting turns every future grant
into a grant to every agent.

209
00:13:20.338 --> 00:13:23.026
And no agent holds a
standing privileged role.

210
00:13:23.026 --> 00:13:26.762
If it seems to need one permanently,
that's a design defect.

211
00:13:26.762 --> 00:13:28.380
Next, policy.

212
00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:35.180
Conditional Access follows
the token's subject,

213
00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:37.468
so one policy rarely covers an agent.

214
00:13:37.468 --> 00:13:41.130
For autonomous agents, the
recommended pattern is simple:

215
00:13:41.130 --> 00:13:44.868
block every agent identity
except the ones you've approved.

216
00:13:44.868 --> 00:13:49.174
The program builds that in report-only
mode and sets the approval tag only

217
00:13:49.174 --> 00:13:53.904
during the production handshake, so the
report shows every agent that skipped it.

218
00:13:53.904 --> 00:13:58.767
It stays in report-only until the agent
targets leave preview in your cloud.

219
00:13:58.767 --> 00:14:03.456
Plan around the known gaps, like the
token exchange step that policy can't see.

220
00:14:03.456 --> 00:14:06.894
And when risk detection flags
an agent, tell its sponsor too,

221
00:14:06.894 --> 00:14:09.280
because they know what normal looks like.

222
00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:11.330
Now, tools and data.

223
00:14:15.080 --> 00:14:18.066
An agent's reach is the sum
of the tools it can call,

224
00:14:18.066 --> 00:14:20.455
so the program draws three boundaries.

225
00:14:20.455 --> 00:14:23.968
First, identity: what the
agent can get a token for.

226
00:14:23.968 --> 00:14:28.046
But the protocol many agent tools
use makes authorization optional,

227
00:14:28.046 --> 00:14:30.193
so some tools sit outside that layer.

228
00:14:30.193 --> 00:14:35.780
Second, a registry of approved tools, so
a call to anything unregistered is drift.

229
00:14:35.780 --> 00:14:40.721
Third, data protection, which isn't
available for agents in GCC yet, so there

230
00:14:40.721 --> 00:14:45.905
your only data boundary is narrow
permissions, and you should record that gap.

231
00:14:45.905 --> 00:14:50.605
Anything outside all three, like
a raw key, becomes a finding.

232
00:14:50.605 --> 00:14:52.550
That takes us to monitoring.

233
00:14:56.300 --> 00:14:59.137
Module 4 is about watching and proving.

234
00:14:59.137 --> 00:15:03.932
We'll find where AI shows up in the
logs, hunt shadow AI on four surfaces,

235
00:15:03.932 --> 00:15:07.212
turn gaps into findings,
run the inventory scan,

236
00:15:07.212 --> 00:15:12.200
and package the evidence an
authorizing official actually needs.

237
00:15:15.950 --> 00:15:19.426
Monitoring starts with knowing
where AI shows up in the logs,

238
00:15:19.426 --> 00:15:21.650
and that's less obvious than you'd think.

239
00:15:21.650 --> 00:15:24.312
An AI built on an ordinary app or managed

240
00:15:24.312 --> 00:15:27.538
identity looks exactly
like any other workload.

241
00:15:27.538 --> 00:15:30.762
Nothing in the log says
it's AI or who sponsors it.

242
00:15:30.762 --> 00:15:32.713
Only your identity record can.

243
00:15:32.713 --> 00:15:37.150
Agent ID objects are easier to spot,
but some logs still don't separate

244
00:15:37.150 --> 00:15:41.900
agents from apps, so your detections
belong on the sign-in and audit tables.

245
00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:46.875
Alert on things that happen outside your
pipeline, like new credentials or grants.

246
00:15:46.875 --> 00:15:51.662
And export everything on day one,
because history starts when export does.

247
00:15:51.662 --> 00:15:54.000
Next, what nobody declared.

248
00:15:57.750 --> 00:16:00.924
For AI, a lot of what
matters was never declared,

249
00:16:00.924 --> 00:16:04.600
so discovery has to be standing,
not a one-time cleanup.

250
00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:08.578
Shadow AI shows up in four
places: services people use,

251
00:16:08.578 --> 00:16:14.725
apps holding consented access, agents on
endpoints, and AI built in your own clouds.

252
00:16:14.725 --> 00:16:17.812
Each needs a different tool,
and none sees everything.

253
00:16:17.812 --> 00:16:22.151
The test is the same everywhere:
does this match an identity record?

254
00:16:22.151 --> 00:16:25.375
Match on the record's key,
never on a display name.

255
00:16:25.375 --> 00:16:27.012
And remember the blind spot.

256
00:16:27.012 --> 00:16:30.605
An agent using a raw key produces
no identity signal at all,

257
00:16:30.605 --> 00:16:34.049
which is why endpoint and
network views aren't optional.

258
00:16:34.049 --> 00:16:35.780
Now, the findings.

259
00:16:39.530 --> 00:16:43.905
Every gap becomes a finding with
a class, a severity and an owner.

260
00:16:43.905 --> 00:16:46.736
The top one is the
reason this book exists:

261
00:16:46.736 --> 00:16:49.817
an agentic system with
no governed identity.

262
00:16:49.817 --> 00:16:53.534
It's the highest priority, and
it's never fixed automatically,

263
00:16:53.534 --> 00:16:56.877
because the right answer
might be issuing an identity,

264
00:16:56.877 --> 00:17:00.593
disabling the agent, or fixing
a mislabeled inventory entry.

265
00:17:00.593 --> 00:17:02.856
That's a decision, not a repair.

266
00:17:02.856 --> 00:17:07.110
A missing authorization starts lower
because the inventory can be wrong,

267
00:17:07.110 --> 00:17:09.342
then rises once the records confirm it.

268
00:17:09.342 --> 00:17:13.885
Each finding becomes one evidence
record, keyed on the identity record,

269
00:17:13.885 --> 00:17:17.118
and it closes only when a
later look shows it's fixed.

270
00:17:17.118 --> 00:17:18.950
Next, the scan.

271
00:17:22.700 --> 00:17:26.537
The scan is cheap because it reads
answers agencies already gave.

272
00:17:26.537 --> 00:17:29.007
Load one year's inventory
as a fixed input,

273
00:17:29.007 --> 00:17:32.275
and report rows you can't read
instead of skipping them.

274
00:17:32.275 --> 00:17:35.907
Then run two kinds of checks:
some on inventory answers alone,

275
00:17:35.907 --> 00:17:38.812
and the important ones
that need your directory.

276
00:17:38.812 --> 00:17:42.776
On the public file, most live
entries trip at least one check,

277
00:17:42.776 --> 00:17:47.063
but those are answers to reconcile,
not findings and not systems.

278
00:17:47.063 --> 00:17:51.322
Prioritize agentic first, route
every row to a named person,

279
00:17:51.322 --> 00:17:54.538
and close only when a
later look shows the fix.

280
00:17:54.538 --> 00:17:57.325
Then each year, compare the two vintages.

281
00:17:57.325 --> 00:17:59.120
Could this run centrally?

282
00:18:02.870 --> 00:18:07.795
In principle, one operator could run the
answer checks for every agency at once.

283
00:18:07.795 --> 00:18:11.907
People call that CDM for AI,
but CDM works differently.

284
00:18:11.907 --> 00:18:15.808
Its tools run inside each
agency and summaries flow up.

285
00:18:15.808 --> 00:18:18.232
What carries over is the split between who

286
00:18:18.232 --> 00:18:20.933
sees across agencies and
who fixes inside them.

287
00:18:20.933 --> 00:18:25.254
A central scan sees only what was
published, so it can't tell whether a

288
00:18:25.254 --> 00:18:29.883
credential exists, rotated or was revoked,
and it can't see shadow AI at all.

289
00:18:29.883 --> 00:18:34.632
That last tier, governing the credential
itself, belongs to each agency.

290
00:18:34.632 --> 00:18:37.132
A central scan starts the conversation.

291
00:18:37.132 --> 00:18:38.432
It doesn't finish it.

292
00:18:38.432 --> 00:18:40.060
Now, the package.

293
00:18:43.810 --> 00:18:47.531
Everything ends in the authorization
package, because that's what

294
00:18:47.531 --> 00:18:51.185
the authorizing official reads,
and only they can accept the risk.

295
00:18:51.185 --> 00:18:55.114
First ask where the AI runs:
inside an existing system,

296
00:18:55.114 --> 00:18:58.597
as a new one, or on a
provider's certified service.

297
00:18:58.597 --> 00:19:02.936
Even then, the identities that service
creates in your tenant are yours.

298
00:19:02.936 --> 00:19:06.642
Then the package should answer
five questions: who owns it,

299
00:19:06.642 --> 00:19:11.585
how it authenticates, what it can reach,
who's watching, and when its access ends.

300
00:19:11.585 --> 00:19:14.010
One control deserves special attention.

301
00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:16.999
Service authentication
isn't in any baseline,

302
00:19:16.999 --> 00:19:20.460
so a plan built only from
the baseline never checks it.

303
00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:21.385
Tailor it in.

304
00:19:21.385 --> 00:19:23.800
Next, format and clocks.

305
00:19:27.550 --> 00:19:29.794
The package should be machine-readable,

306
00:19:29.794 --> 00:19:33.125
and the current version of
OSCAL needs nothing AI-specific.

307
00:19:33.125 --> 00:19:38.574
The AI system is a component, each identity
it uses is a user with its privileges,

308
00:19:38.574 --> 00:19:41.425
and the sponsor and owner are named roles.

309
00:19:41.425 --> 00:19:44.880
Just remember that privileges
in the plan are a description;

310
00:19:44.880 --> 00:19:47.575
the proof is an observation
from the directory.

311
00:19:47.575 --> 00:19:52.313
AI adds a few clocks beside annual
security testing: the yearly inventory,

312
00:19:52.313 --> 00:19:56.663
a compliance plan every two years,
and annual waiver certification.

313
00:19:56.663 --> 00:20:00.438
And NIST's AI overlays aren't
drafted yet, so don't wait.

314
00:20:00.438 --> 00:20:03.750
Tailor your baseline now and
compare when they arrive.

315
00:20:03.750 --> 00:20:05.820
Let's close with decisions.

316
00:20:09.570 --> 00:20:11.895
Every decision here has one owner.

317
00:20:11.895 --> 00:20:14.563
The Chief AI Officer requires every live

318
00:20:14.563 --> 00:20:17.545
inventory entry to link
to an identity record.

319
00:20:17.545 --> 00:20:20.162
The identity governance officer makes sure

320
00:20:20.162 --> 00:20:22.920
that record exists before
any credential does.

321
00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:26.374
The CISO sets the credential,
policy and logging rules,

322
00:20:26.374 --> 00:20:29.152
the system owner decides
what an AI may reach,

323
00:20:29.152 --> 00:20:33.883
and the authorizing official decides
when a change needs reauthorization.

324
00:20:33.883 --> 00:20:39.058
And you can start Monday without buying
anything: list your agents and their owners,

325
00:20:39.058 --> 00:20:42.883
scan your own inventory, build
the block policy in report-only,

326
00:20:42.883 --> 00:20:46.333
export your logs, and tailor
service authentication in.

327
00:20:46.333 --> 00:20:48.507
An agent that acts is an account.

328
00:20:48.507 --> 00:20:50.282
Give it an owner and an end.

329
00:20:50.282 --> 00:20:51.110
Thank you.
